              <h3>Purpose of feedback</h3>
<p>Please see the following video on the purpose of using feedback to develop communication skills.</p>

              <!-- sections.njk VIDEO! -->
              
              
              
      <div class="l-video-container">
        <video poster="~/images/svg/poster.svg" controls preload="metadata" aria-labelledby="watch" width="1000"
          data-base="uc_u5_s2_p1_feedback_purpose" 
          data-ext=".mp4" 
          data-multi="false"
          data-trans="uc_u5_s2_p1_feedback_purpose undefined">
          <source src="https://kpcontent.blob.core.windows.net/$web/resources/v2l/720/uc_u5_s2_p1_feedback_purpose.mp4" type="video/mp4">
          <track default label="English" kind="captions" srclang="en" src="~/videos/captions/vtt/uc_u5_s2_p1_feedback_purpose.vtt">
          <p>Sorry, your browser doesn't support embedded videos</p>
        </video>
      </div>
      
          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Jack:</strong> To improve skills and grow professionally it is important to get feedback especially on how we communicate to others. As we have already said, it helps us to know that others understand what we are communicating, enabling them to respond.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Feedback is a vital part of any communication process and project managers, team leaders, instructors and coaches develop the skill of giving feedback throughout their career. They are also involved in receiving feedback as well as this is an important way of sharing information and communicating within a work environment.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> Feedback should be seen as a positive thing as it can be used to develop communication skills. Feedback should be listened to actively and analysed to help improve future communications. It can provide positive criticism and allows everyone to improve their focus. It can also create a healthy communication flow up and down the levels of an organisation.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Constructive feedback is an excellent tool which can help optimise the communication process and increase transparency and trust. Clear and honest communication flow within a team, saves time and subsequently money and can reduce errors caused by miscommunication to a minimum.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> By seeing that the feedback is there to help projects succeed, it can also motivate the team members. Readily seeking feedback and ways to improve, can help show the openness to other ideas that may be beneficial. It can also showcase unforeseen problems and suggest alternative solutions.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> But saying that feedback that is negative or feedback that is not specific can be unhelpful as it can demotivate. The feedback should not just dismiss the communication but should start the process of engagement and debate.</p>
<p>Those giving feedback especially in a work-based situation should themselves become part of the problem-solving process if the opportunity arises.</p>
<p><strong>Jack:</strong> As we have previously discussed listening is an important part of the communication process and listening and acting on feedback gives opportunities for everyone to grow their skills.</p>
<p>Feedback is essential in communication to know whether the recipient has understood the message in the same terms as intended by the sender and whether he agrees to that message or not.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Feedback also enables us to evaluate the effectiveness of our message and if that feedback is timely then further communications can be made to help clarify any miscommunication or emissions.</p>
<p>Therefore, providing immediate feedback enables people to better learn from any mistakes they make. However, if they communicate effectively to begin with, it's still valuable to provide feedback instantly, as it reinforces that effectiveness and motivates by affirming competence.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
